control 'CKV_AWS_376' do
  title 'Ensure AWS Elastic Load Balancer listener uses TLS/SSL'

  desc <<~DESC
    Checkov asserts this against Terraform. This profile asserts it against
    the aws_elb resources that actually exist, enumerated through the
    declarative API spec.

    Asserts the front-end protocol on the listeners AWS is actually serving,
    including listeners added outside Terraform. Deliberately does NOT also
    assert that HTTPS listeners carry a certificate: ELB classic will not
    create one without SSLCertificateId, so that clause could not fail and
    is not written.
  DESC

  desc 'rationale', <<~RATIONALE
    A listener is where a client's connection lands, so its protocol decides
    whether the session between the client and the boundary is encrypted and
    whether the client can authenticate the endpoint at all. An HTTP or TCP
    listener carries credentials, session cookies and payloads in cleartext
    across whatever network the client sits on, and no configuration behind
    the load balancer compensates for that.
  RATIONALE

  desc 'check', <<~CHECK
    Checkov looks for: aws_elb: a Classic Load Balancer listener whose
    front-end Protocol is anything other than HTTPS or SSL — that is, HTTP
    or raw TCP.
  CHECK

  desc 'fix', <<~'FIX'
    Terraform — aws_elb:

      resource "aws_elb" "example" {
        name    = "example"
        subnets = var.public_subnet_ids

        listener {
          lb_port            = 443
          lb_protocol        = "https"
          instance_port      = 8443
          instance_protocol  = "https"
          ssl_certificate_id = aws_acm_certificate.example.arn
        }

        # There is no lb_protocol = "http" listener. A redirect from 80 is not
        # expressible on a Classic Load Balancer, so if one is needed the
        # right move is an Application Load Balancer with a redirect action,
        # not an HTTP listener here.
      }

    Out of band — aws_elb:

      aws elb create-load-balancer-listeners --load-balancer-name <name> --listeners Protocol=HTTPS,LoadBalancerPort=443,InstanceProtocol=HTTPS,InstancePort=8443,SSLCertificateId=<acm-arn>
      aws elb delete-load-balancer-listeners --load-balancer-name <name> --load-balancer-ports 80

    Note (aws_elb): Create the HTTPS listener before deleting the cleartext one,
    and move clients over before the delete — deleting a listener drops every
    connection on that port immediately and there is no redirect to catch them.
    Classic Load Balancers cannot redirect at all, so a workload that needs an
    HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect is a migration to an Application Load Balancer, not a
    listener edit. Check the instance_protocol leg too: it is a separate
    setting, not covered by this control, and an HTTPS front end talking
    cleartext to the instances leaves the inside of the VPC unencrypted.
  FIX

  tag checkov_id:            'CKV_AWS_376'
  tag checkov_category:      'NETWORKING'
  tag checkov_version:       '3.3.16'
  tag checkov_kind:          'custom'
  tag tf_resources:          %w[aws_elb]
  tag tf_docs:               'https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/aws/latest/docs/resources/elb'
  tag nist:                  ['SC-8', 'SC-8 (1)']
  tag nist_r4:               ['SC-8', 'SC-8 (1)']
  tag cci:                   ['CCI-002418', 'CCI-002421']
